# Redstart Nest — reverse proxy for a level-3 install.
#
# Nest does not do TLS, at any phase, and that is a settled decision rather
# than an omission (design §3.3). It binds loopback by default and speaks plain
# HTTP; encryption belongs to a proxy in front that handles certificates and
# renewal properly. Caddy, nginx and Traefik all do — this is the Caddy one
# because it needs the least configuration to be correct.
#
# On an appliance this ships IN THE IMAGE, preconfigured, so the box arrives
# with working HTTPS and nobody had to know anything.
#
# UNVERIFIED. Written from the design's requirements; nobody has run it against
# a real deployment yet.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pick ONE of the two shapes below. Each has a CONTROL-PLANE block (19083, the
# admin UI) and a DATA-PLANE block (19080, the chat UI and inference, with the
# built-in MCP server's /mcp beside it). They are separate sites on purpose —
# see "THE DATA PLANE IS SEPARATE" at the bottom.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# (1) A REAL DOMAIN. Caddy obtains and renews a publicly trusted certificate
# automatically, and browsers show no warning. Requires the name to resolve to
# this box and ports 80/443 reachable for the challenge.
#
# nest.example.com {
#     reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:19083
# }
#
# chat.example.com {
#     handle /mcp {
#         reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:19082
#     }
#     handle {
#         reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:19080
#     }
# }

# (2) NO DOMAIN — the small-operator case, and the one that looks worst while
# actually being served well. Caddy issues its own certificate; each device
# gets a click-through warning once. Traffic is encrypted either way: the
# warning is about VERIFICATION, not encryption. It fully defeats passive
# capture on the LAN, which is the realistic opportunistic threat; it does not
# defeat an active attacker already able to redirect traffic, who in that
# environment has easier targets.
:443 {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:19083
}

# The data plane, on a port of its own because both sites cannot share :443
# without a hostname to tell them apart. The browser reaches it at
# https://<this-box>:8443. Expect a second click-through: the warning is per
# origin, and this is a different one.
:8443 {
	tls internal

	# The built-in MCP server is a separate listener (gateway port + 2) that the
	# chat UI connects to from the BROWSER, not through the gateway. Serving it
	# on the same origin keeps it inside the TLS and avoids a mixed-content
	# block. It answers /mcp and nothing else, so the exact path is the whole
	# route. See "TOOLS IN THE CHAT UI" below for how the chat UI learns it.
	handle /mcp {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:19082
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:19080
	}
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What Nest expects of whatever sits here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE CONTROL PLANE STAYS ON LOOPBACK. Leave Nest's bind address at 127.0.0.1
# and let the proxy be the only thing on the network. If the control plane is
# bound to a LAN address as well, the proxy is decoration — anyone can reach
# :19083 directly, in the clear.
#
# NEST SEES PROXIED REQUESTS AS ARRIVING FROM LOOPBACK, and that is harmless
# here ONLY because of two properties that must not be quietly changed:
# there is no localhost exemption anywhere in authentication (auth.mjs), and
# control-plane access is owner-password-only (decision 18). Do not add
# IP-based rules later without revisiting that — "requests from this machine
# are trusted" would, behind this proxy, mean "requests from anywhere are
# trusted", and Nest also runs third-party plugin code locally.
#
# X-Forwarded-For IS DELIBERATELY NOT TRUSTED by Nest. A consequence worth
# knowing rather than working around: rate limiting is keyed on the remote
# address, so behind this proxy every caller shares one bucket. Treat that
# limit as a brake on automated guessing, not as an access control. Caddy can
# apply its own per-client limits if that matters for a deployment.
#
# THE DATA PLANE IS SEPARATE. The gateway (19080) serves inference and the chat
# UI and has its own auth setting, so it gets its own site rather than a path
# under the control plane's. Two sites keep "who can reach the admin UI" a
# question with its own answer: drop the control-plane block and the chat
# stays up, and the reverse holds too.
#
# THE DATA PLANE STAYS ON LOOPBACK TOO. Leave network mode OFF. Network mode
# binds the gateway and the MCP server to every interface, and then 19080 and
# 19082 answer in the clear beside this proxy — the same decoration problem as
# above. It also starts the Windows clean-URL proxy on port 80, which Caddy
# needs for HTTP->HTTPS redirects and, with a real domain, the ACME challenge.
#
# STREAMING. Completions, the MCP transport and the live event feed are all
# server-sent events. Caddy flushes text/event-stream responses immediately by
# default, so no flush_interval is set here. If replies arrive all at once
# instead of token by token, this is the first thing to suspect.
#
# THE GATEWAY IS UP ONLY WHILE A MODEL IS RUNNING. With no model loaded, the
# data-plane site answers 502. That is Caddy reporting nothing behind it, not a
# proxy fault.
#
# RATE LIMITING ON THE CHAT LOGIN. The shared-bucket point above matters more
# here than on the control plane: every account's login attempts, from every
# device, land in the one bucket. Stock Caddy has no rate-limit directive (it
# is a third-party module), so this file sets none.
#
# TOOLS IN THE CHAT UI. The gateway tells the chat UI where the built-in MCP
# server is (GET /redstart/mcp-servers), and the browser connects to that URL
# itself. When the request carries X-Forwarded-Proto, which Caddy sets on
# every proxied request, Nest advertises <proto>://<host>/mcp: this site's own
# origin, which the /mcp route above serves. Without it, Nest advertises
# http://<host>:19082/mcp, which an https page will not call. If the built-in
# tools do not connect, open /redstart/mcp-servers and check which URL came
# back. builtInMcpUrl() in tools-gateway.mjs is the code.
